So we need to count how many factors of each prime number exist in $942!$: let $p$ be a prime number, then the total number of factors of $p$ are equal to $n_p = \sum_{i = 1}^\infty\left[{\left\lfloor{\frac{942}{p^i}}\right\rfloor}\right]$. Moreover, for every prime number $p$, we have the equality ${\textstyle n_p}={\textstyle n_{942-p}},{\text{ }}p\neq 2$. Combining these restrictions, it suffices to consider $p\leq 47$ Now, let's take $p={3}$: ${\textstyle n_3}={\textstyle \left\lfloor{\frac{942}{3}}\right\rfloor}+{\textstyle \left\lfloor{\frac{942}{3^2}}\right\rfloor}+{\textstyle \left\lfloor{\frac{942}{3^3}}\right\rfloor}+{\textstyle \left\lfloor{\frac{942}{3^4}}\right\rfloor}+{\textstyle \left\lfloor{\frac{942}{3^5}}\right\rfloor}=314+104+34+11+3=466$. Similarly, we have ${\textstyle n_5}=219},{\text{ }}{\textstyle n_7}=115},{\text{ }}
${\displaystyle n_{11}=85},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{13}}={\text{ }72},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{17}}={\text{ }56},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{19}}={\text{ }52},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{23}}={\text{ }44},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{29}}={\text{ }32},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{31}}={\text{ }31},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{37}}={\text{ }}26},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{41}}={\text{ }}23},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{43}}={\text{ }}22},{\text{ }}
${\text{ }}{\textstyle n_{47}}={\text{ }}20}$. Thus, the answer is ${\text{ }min\{466,219,115,85,72,56,52,44,32,31,26,23,22,20\}=20}.$</think> Answer: $20$

